      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We would like to subtract 6 from each term in order to complete the square.\nFirst, we complete the square on the terms involving $x$: \\begin{align*}\nx^2 + 6x &= (x^2 + 6x + \\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2) - \\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^2 - 9.\n\\end{align*}\nNext, we complete the square on the terms involving $y$: \\begin{align*}\n2y^2 - 4y &= 2(y^2 - 2y) \\\\\n&= 2(y^2 - 2y + \\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right)^2) - 2\\left(\\frac{2}{2}\\right)^2 \\\\\n&= 2(y - 1)^2 - 2.\n\\end{align*}\nFinally, we combine all our terms: \\begin{align*}\nz &= (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11.\n\\end{align*}\nSince the square of any real number cannot be negative, the minimum value of $z$ is ${11}$ when $(x + 3)^2 = 0$ and $(y - 1)^2 = 0$.\nThus, the minimum value of $z$ is $\\boxed{11}$.The answer is: 11",
